[description]

[b]Azure Pipelines[/b] defines CI/CD in YAML checked into the repo (or classic UI definitions in older setups). [b]Stages[/b] group jobs; [b]jobs[/b] target agent pools; [b]steps[/b] run tasks (bash, PowerShell, marketplace tasks). Conditional logic, templates, and parameterization reduce copy-paste across microservices.

[b]Agents:[/b] Microsoft-hosted agents are convenient but generic; self-hosted pools give GPU access, private networks, and compliance boundaries at the cost of patching and trust. Agent authentication uses keys or PAT-like tokens—rotate on departure.

[b]Environments and deployments:[/b] environments wrap deployment targets with approvals, branch filters, and checks (invoke REST gates, delay, etc.). They map human release process onto pipeline YAML.

[b]Service connections:[/b] store cloud credentials for deploy steps—scope narrowly (subscription + resource group) and audit usage.

[b]Artifacts:[/b] pipeline runs publish packages, symbols, and test results consumed by releases or other pipelines—set retention to control cost.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] treat agents like cattle in the cloud but pets on-prem: both need health checks, capacity planning, and secret hygiene.
